784
00:31:51,050 --> 00:31:53,380
This cup is removed, and we have to drink.

785
00:31:53,880 --> 00:31:55,390
Cheers!

786
00:31:58,150 --> 00:31:59,260
Did you play?

787
00:32:00,860 --> 00:32:02,360
(We won.)

788
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:04,430
That was fun.

789
00:32:04,430 --> 00:32:05,900
- Let's go!
- Yes.

790
00:32:05,900 --> 00:32:07,360
- Let's go.
- Let's go.

791
00:32:07,360 --> 00:32:08,500
- Go.
- Let's go!

792
00:32:08,500 --> 00:32:11,200
(They enjoy the music even on their way back.)

793
00:32:12,340 --> 00:32:13,840
Resist you

794
00:32:14,040 --> 00:32:15,310
Mamma mia

795
00:32:17,870 --> 00:32:20,140
I don't think I could've done the pedal pub in Korea.

796
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:22,010
Honestly, I could do it because I was abroad.

797
00:32:22,310 --> 00:32:24,610
I didn't feel embarrassed. It was really great.

798
00:32:25,380 --> 00:32:27,720
It was a whole new experience.

799
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:30,690
How can they block off a single lane?

800
00:32:31,020 --> 00:32:32,420
That was fascinating.

801
00:32:32,890 --> 00:32:34,490
I had really experienced that...

802
00:32:34,490 --> 00:32:36,830
in a new environment.

803
00:32:37,130 --> 00:32:39,430
It was so fun, and I adapted quickly.

804
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:41,600
They were all things I liked,

805
00:32:41,660 --> 00:32:42,870
so it was really nice.

806
00:32:53,310 --> 00:32:54,480
It's crazy.

807
00:32:55,040 --> 00:32:57,210
(I'm crazy excited!)

808
00:33:07,060 --> 00:33:08,120
Let's go.

809
00:33:19,670 --> 00:33:22,440
You have to sacrifice warmth for fashion.

810
00:33:24,410 --> 00:33:26,310
Forget about the pedal pub earlier.

811
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:28,440
That person isn't here anymore.

812
00:33:29,410 --> 00:33:30,510
Yes!

813
00:33:30,510 --> 00:33:33,020
We're showing different sides of ourselves today.

814
00:33:35,050 --> 00:33:38,120
(Forget about what happened earlier. Their unexpected charm comes out!)

815
00:33:38,350 --> 00:33:40,720
Just a few hours ago,

816
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:42,990
we were screaming in the car...

817
00:33:42,990 --> 00:33:45,160
Talk about a day-and-night difference.

818
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:48,360
- Since we're all dressed up, - Yes.

819
00:33:48,870 --> 00:33:50,830
let's eat nice and slow.

820
00:33:51,070 --> 00:33:53,770
Let's take our time to really savour every bite.

821
00:33:54,970 --> 00:33:56,770
That's the restaurant we're going to.

822
00:33:57,510 --> 00:33:58,640
Steak House.

823
00:33:59,580 --> 00:34:00,680
We're here.

824
00:34:00,780 --> 00:34:03,310
Ladies first.

825
00:34:04,780 --> 00:34:05,950
Thank you.

826
00:34:07,120 --> 00:34:08,280
Go on in, gentleman.

827
00:34:09,520 --> 00:34:10,790
Ladies and gentlemen.

828
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:13,520
Okay.

829
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:17,730
It looks expensive.

830
00:34:19,960 --> 00:34:23,530
So you can't enter this place unless you dress up, right?

831
00:34:23,530 --> 00:34:24,530
That's right.

832
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:27,170
Thank you.

833
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:29,210
I'm like the manager here.

834
00:34:32,340 --> 00:34:33,440
Hello.

835
00:34:34,780 --> 00:34:36,410
He's wearing a hoodie.

836
00:34:36,410 --> 00:34:38,410
What? What are we doing?

837
00:34:39,320 --> 00:34:40,780
We can also wear our usual clothes.

838
00:34:40,980 --> 00:34:42,080
Right.

839
00:34:45,390 --> 00:34:46,390
(The place Hyun Moo led them...)

840
00:34:46,390 --> 00:34:47,520
This way.

841
00:34:47,520 --> 00:34:48,660
Thank you.

842
00:34:49,660 --> 00:34:52,630
(A beloved Calgary restaurant...)

843
00:34:52,630 --> 00:34:56,570
(that's been around since 1972)

844
00:34:56,830 --> 00:34:59,440
(A place that's been making delicious memories...)

845
00:34:59,440 --> 00:35:01,770
(with the people of Calgary for over 55 years)

846
00:35:02,340 --> 00:35:06,740
(You can see the grill station behind the glass.)

847
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:10,150
(A place to savour Alberta beef...)

848
00:35:10,250 --> 00:35:13,620
(grilled at over 800°C)

849
00:35:14,920 --> 00:35:19,320
(Tonight's special seat even has a front-row view of the flames.)

850
00:35:19,460 --> 00:35:22,260
- The meat...
- Goodness.

851
00:35:22,260 --> 00:35:24,560
They're grilling the meat here.

852
00:35:26,030 --> 00:35:28,130
Touch the glass. How hot is it?

853
00:35:28,130 --> 00:35:29,570
- Is it very hot?
- It's warm.

854
00:35:29,570 --> 00:35:30,700
- It's warm?
- It's warm.

855
00:35:30,830 --> 00:35:32,400
- It's not hot.
- Like the warm spot of the furnace.

856
00:35:32,400 --> 00:35:33,970
It looks like it will be really warm in there.

857
00:35:34,040 --> 00:35:35,970
- It would be hot.
- It would be really hot.

858
00:35:37,110 --> 00:35:39,180
Hey, it's Mr. Yongdaegal.

859
00:35:39,940 --> 00:35:43,980
Alberta is one of the biggest cattle-producing regions in Canada.

860
00:35:44,050 --> 00:35:46,250
As of July 2024,

861
00:35:46,250 --> 00:35:49,090
there were about 5.1 million cattle...

862
00:35:49,090 --> 00:35:51,120
and calves being raised here.

863
00:35:51,290 --> 00:35:55,160
That's about 43 percent of all the cattle in Canada.

864
00:35:55,660 --> 00:35:59,100
Alberta beef has earned a reputation around the world...

865
00:35:59,230 --> 00:36:01,930
for its quality and rich flavour.

866
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:07,200
(Alberta beef is a must-eat when on a Calgary trip.)

867
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:10,840
Is there a specific cut you like?

868
00:36:11,610 --> 00:36:13,080
- No.
- No.

869
00:36:13,780 --> 00:36:16,150
(They just like beef.)

870
00:36:16,250 --> 00:36:19,020
When you order a T-bone steak, you see that T-shaped bone.

871
00:36:19,450 --> 00:36:21,950
This part is tenderloin and this part is sirloin.

872
00:36:22,490 --> 00:36:24,690
- You can taste both parts.
- Yes.

873
00:36:25,390 --> 00:36:26,620
Ribeye...

874
00:36:26,620 --> 00:36:28,320
- You've heard of ribeye, right?
- Yes.

875
00:36:28,490 --> 00:36:30,430
- I'd love that.
- Let's get ribeye...

876
00:36:30,760 --> 00:36:33,400
and red wine that really goes well with it.

877
00:36:33,860 --> 00:36:34,860
Nice.

878
00:36:34,860 --> 00:36:36,300
- Nice.
- A light one.

879
00:36:36,300 --> 00:36:38,270
(Even red wine, the perfect pairing for beef)

880
00:36:38,600 --> 00:36:39,870
Speaking of the menu,

881
00:36:39,870 --> 00:36:41,600
you want to start with some appetizers.

882
00:36:41,770 --> 00:36:43,610
The most famous ones are right here, sir.

883
00:36:43,610 --> 00:36:45,680
- Probably Caesar salad, right?
- Absolutely.

884
00:36:45,680 --> 00:36:47,980
We're going to order...

885
00:36:48,140 --> 00:36:49,350
the Caesar salad.

886
00:36:49,350 --> 00:36:50,950
- Absolutely. Tableside?
- Yes.

887
00:36:51,110 --> 00:36:52,720
- Ribeye.
- Absolutely.

888
00:36:53,020 --> 00:36:55,080
- T-bone steak.
- T-bone, absolutely.

889
00:36:55,180 --> 00:36:56,390
How would you like it cooked?

890
00:36:56,720 --> 00:36:58,920
- I like it medium-rare.
- Medium-rare.

891
00:36:59,020 --> 00:37:00,590
- I'll have it like that too.
- Medium-rare.

892
00:37:01,220 --> 00:37:02,220
Yes.

893
00:37:02,220 --> 00:37:04,990
I'll have one onion soup as well.

894
00:37:05,230 --> 00:37:07,430
- Sure. Soup.
- Perfect.

895
00:37:07,500 --> 00:37:08,730
We're good for now.

896
00:37:08,730 --> 00:37:10,870
Yes. I'll get you started with some cheese and garlic toast.

897
00:37:10,870 --> 00:37:12,200
- Good.
- Perfect.

898
00:37:12,200 --> 00:37:13,470
Thank you so much.

899
00:37:16,540 --> 00:37:18,940
Cheese and garlic toast right here for you.

900
00:37:20,880 --> 00:37:23,450
The garlic one, and the second one is going to be cheese.

901
00:37:25,180 --> 00:37:26,280
Please enjoy.

902
00:37:28,450 --> 00:37:33,220
(They get their appetites going with some warm bread.)

903
00:37:36,430 --> 00:37:39,700
Actually, I don't go to such steakhouses...

904
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:42,000
with guys.

905
00:37:42,000 --> 00:37:43,130
- You wouldn't.
- I really don't.

906
00:37:43,130 --> 00:37:44,530
You just have pork belly with guys.

907
00:37:44,900 --> 00:37:47,470
But you come to such places for a blind date...

908
00:37:48,670 --> 00:37:50,970
- Or an anniversary.
- Or an anniversary.

909
00:37:51,310 --> 00:37:53,310
I don't dress up so much like this, but I still dress up,

910
00:37:54,010 --> 00:37:55,410
slice the steak, chat,

911
00:37:55,850 --> 00:37:56,980
and have some wine.

912
00:37:57,080 --> 00:37:59,220
- That would be romantic.
- It is.

913
00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:00,280
That's right.

914
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:03,890
(An atmosphere you'd want when celebrating a special day)

915
00:38:04,250 --> 00:38:07,790
Since we're so dressed up,

916
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:09,660
- I can't really talk so much.
- Just go ahead.

917
00:38:09,890 --> 00:38:11,860
It's okay. It's just us here.

918
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:13,400
Say everything you want to say.

919
00:38:13,500 --> 00:38:15,100
We got dressed up just so we're not stopped at the entrance.

920
00:38:16,570 --> 00:38:18,330
We got dressed up to get through that door.

921
00:38:18,330 --> 00:38:19,640
(They now know why they got dressed up.)

922
00:38:19,840 --> 00:38:21,670
House-made caramelized onion soups.

923
00:38:21,970 --> 00:38:23,110
Onion soup.

924
00:38:24,110 --> 00:38:28,210
(Warm onion soup with a rich flavour)

925
00:38:29,510 --> 00:38:30,680
Nice.

926
00:38:32,950 --> 00:38:35,420
It's like doenjang soup.

927
00:38:35,550 --> 00:38:37,050
With makjang.

928
00:38:37,050 --> 00:38:39,260
It's very Korean.

929
00:38:40,220 --> 00:38:41,520
How is the taste so rich and savoury?

930
00:38:41,520 --> 00:38:43,430
It would be perfect with rice.

931
00:38:43,430 --> 00:38:45,390
- With dried laver.
- It tastes like meat soup.

932
00:38:49,430 --> 00:38:52,230
It's like a hangover soup.

933
00:38:52,870 --> 00:38:55,440
It would be like doenjang soup when you add tofu here.

934
00:38:57,140 --> 00:39:00,340
(Instead of warm doenjang soup,)

935
00:39:00,340 --> 00:39:03,080
(they're curing the hangover with onion soup.)

936
00:39:03,810 --> 00:39:05,150
This is nice.

937
00:39:11,020 --> 00:39:12,920
Do you know how you two look like to me?

938
00:39:14,090 --> 00:39:15,360
Like my son and daughter I raised so well.

939
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:16,360
(Son, daughter, dad)

940
00:39:16,730 --> 00:39:19,200
Like I'm saying "Well done. You graduated."

941
00:39:19,700 --> 00:39:21,600
"Let me treat you to a meal. You should eat at such places too."

942
00:39:21,760 --> 00:39:24,030
- "Since you've become adults..." - When is Mom coming?

943
00:39:24,170 --> 00:39:25,470
When is Mom coming?

944
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:27,700
She ran away.

945
00:39:28,340 --> 00:39:29,440
She ran away.

946
00:39:30,010 --> 00:39:31,670
Don't talk about her in front of me. It annoys me.

947
00:39:32,510 --> 00:39:33,640
(It's a shocking twist.)

948
00:39:33,640 --> 00:39:34,780
It's really annoying.

949
00:39:34,780 --> 00:39:36,050
(It's a shocking twist.)

950
00:39:36,050 --> 00:39:38,280
How are you so good when you don't have experience?

951
00:39:39,250 --> 00:39:40,950
I seem like a bachelor dad.

952
00:39:41,350 --> 00:39:42,420
Yes.

953
00:39:42,690 --> 00:39:45,620
Say someone who is to be your son-in-law...

954
00:39:45,820 --> 00:39:49,190
came to greet you and talk about marriage.

955
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:50,790
How would you feel when he knows nothing?

956
00:39:50,790 --> 00:39:51,990
He just says, "The food is nice."

957
00:39:52,230 --> 00:39:54,960
I'd think he'd really make my daughter happy.

958
00:39:55,100 --> 00:39:56,170
Really?

959
00:39:56,170 --> 00:39:57,870
It's better to have no preferences than to be stubborn.

960
00:39:58,030 --> 00:39:59,270
- Really?
- Right.

961
00:39:59,540 --> 00:40:01,370
"You don't have to know it. Just go ahead and eat."

962
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:03,110
"My daughter would know well."

963
00:40:03,770 --> 00:40:05,610
- Even your daughter doesn't know.
- I'm the daughter.

964
00:40:05,810 --> 00:40:07,810
- Then I'll teach you.
- The dad?

965
00:40:07,810 --> 00:40:10,480
Try having this with your husband later.

966
00:40:10,480 --> 00:40:11,650
I know a lot about food.

967
00:40:11,810 --> 00:40:13,220
I'd also have fun introducing you to new things.

968
00:40:14,350 --> 00:40:17,150
We'll be starting our famous tableside Caesar.

969
00:40:17,790 --> 00:40:19,050
This is what...

970
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:20,320
- I really wanted to see.
- Absolutely.

971
00:40:20,560 --> 00:40:23,560
Just like in the 1920s...

972
00:40:23,560 --> 00:40:24,830
when the Caesar salad was created,

973
00:40:24,830 --> 00:40:26,900
we're trying to replicate the original recipe.

974
00:40:27,230 --> 00:40:29,630
We're using some chives, some croutons,

975
00:40:29,930 --> 00:40:31,830
some Grana Padano cheese, lemon juice,

976
00:40:31,830 --> 00:40:34,970
the whole egg yolk, and of course, our garlic and anchovy.

977
00:40:35,340 --> 00:40:37,140
Starting with some freshly ground pepper.

978
00:40:37,140 --> 00:40:39,140
That pepper grinder is huge.

979
00:40:39,580 --> 00:40:40,940
You definitely need pepper.

980
00:40:41,040 --> 00:40:43,180
A Caesar salad is basically half pepper.

981
00:40:43,480 --> 00:40:44,580
Really?

982
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:47,950
And we're going to be using our Caesar specialty salt.

983
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:51,450
That's a very unique one. It contains more than 14 spices.

984
00:40:52,320 --> 00:40:54,620
And we're going to be using our garlic and anchovy.

985
00:40:54,990 --> 00:40:56,960
And right now, the next step is called emulsification.

986
00:40:56,960 --> 00:40:59,130
We're going to be doing some emulsification with oil.

987
00:41:01,530 --> 00:41:04,970
Right. So you saw how liquid-like the dressing was in the beginning.

988
00:41:04,970 --> 00:41:06,700
It's very thick now.

989
00:41:07,300 --> 00:41:09,100
Romaine lettuce. Absolutely correct.

990
00:41:09,910 --> 00:41:11,610
So that's how it's made.

991
00:41:12,470 --> 00:41:17,050
(Fresh romaine lettuce is evenly coated in a rich, savoury dressing.)

992
00:41:17,150 --> 00:41:20,650
What I heard is, in the 1920s,

993
00:41:21,150 --> 00:41:24,150
in the United States, they banned...

994
00:41:24,350 --> 00:41:26,560
importing alcohol and liquor.

995
00:41:26,890 --> 00:41:29,430
So many Americans moved to Mexico.

996
00:41:29,430 --> 00:41:31,290
- Mexico. Yes.
- So...

997
00:41:31,290 --> 00:41:32,900
Because legally, there was a prohibition.

998
00:41:32,900 --> 00:41:34,460
So legally, you couldn't drink.

999
00:41:34,460 --> 00:41:36,430
That's why so many Americans...

1000
00:41:36,430 --> 00:41:38,170
- got together...
- They went to Tijuana.

1001
00:41:38,170 --> 00:41:40,270
They ran out of food and...

1002
00:41:40,270 --> 00:41:43,170
Yes, and then they would... The chef decided to create a salad.

1003
00:41:43,170 --> 00:41:46,140
He still had some ingredients, and he created Caesar salad.

1004
00:41:47,110 --> 00:41:48,110
What cheese is it?

1005
00:41:48,110 --> 00:41:49,550
Grana Padano cheese.

1006
00:41:51,150 --> 00:41:52,650
- That looks great.
- So much effort is put...

1007
00:41:52,650 --> 00:41:54,150
in making a salad.

1008
00:41:54,620 --> 00:41:56,250
Have you ever seen this?

1009
00:41:56,250 --> 00:41:58,520
I've seen salad like that many times.

1010
00:41:58,520 --> 00:42:00,460
But I don't know if they were Caesar salads.

1011
00:42:00,460 --> 00:42:02,020
You'd tell that it tastes different.

1012
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:04,560
- Really?
- You'll taste the cheese.

1013
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:06,900
I'll know it when I try it.

1014
00:42:07,900 --> 00:42:09,670
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

1015
00:42:10,430 --> 00:42:12,300
Right here is...

1016
00:42:12,300 --> 00:42:13,840
It's really fresh.

1017
00:42:14,270 --> 00:42:15,300
Thank you.

1018
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:16,470
Please enjoy.

1019
00:42:18,410 --> 00:42:20,010
- I'll know when I try it.
- It's just amazing.

1020
00:42:26,120 --> 00:42:27,380
I've had this before.

1021
00:42:28,480 --> 00:42:29,850
This...

1022
00:42:30,290 --> 00:42:31,620
I've tried this many times.

1023
00:42:31,850 --> 00:42:32,890
Really?

1024
00:42:32,890 --> 00:42:34,620
I just didn't know its name.

1025
00:42:34,620 --> 00:42:35,760
This is what it was.

1026
00:42:36,660 --> 00:42:37,890
I've tried this many times.

1027
00:42:39,030 --> 00:42:40,130
So this is the original.

1028
00:42:40,130 --> 00:42:42,700
I've had Caesar salads many times.

1029
00:42:44,970 --> 00:42:46,470
- It's tasty.
- It's really good.

1030
00:42:46,700 --> 00:42:50,240
It's quite interesting to eat after seeing the process.

1031
00:42:50,240 --> 00:42:52,070
I'm so thrilled because it was my first time seeing the process.

1032
00:42:52,070 --> 00:42:53,280
So this is how it's made.

1033
00:42:54,010 --> 00:42:57,810
It smells much better, and the cheese flavour isn't so strong.

1034
00:42:57,810 --> 00:42:59,580
- The flavour is less intense.
- Yes.

1035
00:43:02,620 --> 00:43:04,450
That's actually the steaks for you.

1036
00:43:04,450 --> 00:43:05,960
They're ours.

1037
00:43:06,390 --> 00:43:09,090
The one closer to you is the T-bone.

1038
00:43:09,090 --> 00:43:11,730
And closer to the chef is the bone-in ribeye.

1039
00:43:11,960 --> 00:43:13,500
Look at them.

1040
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:15,600
The temperature inside...

1041
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:16,870
is 65°C.

1042
00:43:16,870 --> 00:43:18,030
Goodness.

1043
00:43:18,930 --> 00:43:21,300
It's 65°C inside, and the fire is 800°C.

1044
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:22,440
(The grill is 800°C.)

1045
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:23,610
It would be really hot.

1046
00:43:23,610 --> 00:43:24,840
He keeps going out because it's hot.

1047
00:43:24,840 --> 00:43:26,180
He leaves as soon as he puts the meat on the grill.

1048
00:43:28,610 --> 00:43:30,450
He leaves right after checking the meat since it's hot.

1049
00:43:30,980 --> 00:43:32,550
(It's not an easy task!)

1050
00:43:32,920 --> 00:43:35,250
He puts it on the grill and leaves. It would be hot.

1051
00:43:35,580 --> 00:43:36,590
- He leaves.
- He leaves.

1052
00:43:36,590 --> 00:43:37,820
Oil is splattering everywhere.

1053
00:43:40,690 --> 00:43:41,860
It looks really good.

1054
00:43:43,530 --> 00:43:46,200
(Their steak is ready.)

1055
00:43:47,500 --> 00:43:49,300
The steakhouse dish has arrived.

1056
00:43:49,300 --> 00:43:50,900
We have our T-bone.

1057
00:43:51,870 --> 00:43:52,870
- Seriously.
- We have the tenderloin...

1058
00:43:52,870 --> 00:43:53,870
and New York strip loin.

1059
00:43:53,870 --> 00:43:55,770
And then we have our bone-in ribeye.

1060
00:43:56,710 --> 00:43:59,240
Those are your steaks. Do you want me to cut them right away,

1061
00:43:59,240 --> 00:44:00,940
or do you want to take some pictures?

1062
00:44:01,210 --> 00:44:02,310
(I have to take a picture of this!)

1063
00:44:02,310 --> 00:44:05,310
There's a picture-taking culture here as well.

1064
00:44:05,580 --> 00:44:07,980
I guess it's not just in Korea. I guess everyone does it.

1065
00:44:09,150 --> 00:44:10,250
Since it's a good dish.

1066
00:44:11,920 --> 00:44:15,490
(T-bone steak and ribeye steak)

1067
00:44:17,860 --> 00:44:19,860
A beautiful bone-in ribeye right here.

1068
00:44:20,430 --> 00:44:21,560
Beautiful.

1069
00:44:23,570 --> 00:44:24,700
He's good.

1070
00:44:27,640 --> 00:44:29,370
(The chef's attention turns to the steak.)

1071
00:44:31,040 --> 00:44:32,270
The chef just watched him.

1072
00:44:32,270 --> 00:44:33,880
He's checking if he's slicing well.

1073
00:44:39,650 --> 00:44:42,650
(Even the chef trusts him to cut it perfectly.)

1074
00:44:42,920 --> 00:44:45,090
A beautiful bone-in ribeye right here.

1075
00:44:45,090 --> 00:44:46,120
(It's time to try the perfectly-cut ribeye.)

1076
00:44:47,160 --> 00:44:49,430
You can all start eating. I'll serve it to you.

1077
00:44:49,430 --> 00:44:50,460
Amazing.

1078
00:44:53,030 --> 00:44:54,360
It's soft.

1079
00:44:54,600 --> 00:44:57,070
(The ribeye is so tender, it's easy to slice.)

1080
00:44:57,070 --> 00:44:58,570
The meat is jiggling.

1081
00:45:02,640 --> 00:45:04,010
- Everything?
- Yes.

1082
00:45:07,810 --> 00:45:09,210
It's soft.

1083
00:45:10,880 --> 00:45:12,350
It's really soft.

1084
00:45:13,120 --> 00:45:14,450
- It's so soft.
- Yes.

1085
00:45:14,820 --> 00:45:16,750
It's perfectly seasoned too. It's not too salty.

1086
00:45:16,750 --> 00:45:20,190
(A tender ribeye steak with a rich, deep flavour)

1087
00:45:23,030 --> 00:45:24,530
- It's good.
- It's good.

1088
00:45:25,330 --> 00:45:26,860
- Is it good?
- Yes. It's good.

1089
00:45:27,360 --> 00:45:34,040
(Dinner time, filled with happiness from the delicious steak)

1090
00:45:41,580 --> 00:45:47,220
(It's finally time to taste the T-bone steak.)

1091
00:45:47,720 --> 00:45:49,520
A beautiful T-bone right here.

1092
00:45:49,520 --> 00:45:50,690
Amazing.

1093
00:45:52,050 --> 00:45:53,120
Thank you.

1094
00:45:53,120 --> 00:45:54,520
(T-bone steak: sirloin, tenderloin Ribeye steak: ribeye)

1095
00:45:54,520 --> 00:45:55,820
What was this? The T...

1096
00:45:55,820 --> 00:45:58,090
That's the T-bone steak, and that's the sirloin.

1097
00:45:58,560 --> 00:46:00,760
It's a little lighter.

1098
00:46:00,760 --> 00:46:02,400
(The sirloin is on the right side of the T-shaped bone.)

1099
00:46:15,240 --> 00:46:16,250
Amazing.

1100
00:46:21,150 --> 00:46:22,420
It's got the texture.

1101
00:46:23,690 --> 00:46:26,420
Everyone has their preferred cut.

1102
00:46:26,890 --> 00:46:29,360
If you like soft cuts, you should have the ribeye.

1103
00:46:30,290 --> 00:46:31,630
This is the sirloin.

1104
00:46:38,370 --> 00:46:39,740
This is chewy.

1105
00:46:39,740 --> 00:46:41,140
That's right. It's the sirloin.

1106
00:46:41,440 --> 00:46:43,010
I think I get the difference.

1107
00:46:43,340 --> 00:46:45,270
This goes in better,

1108
00:46:45,270 --> 00:46:46,780
and this doesn't.

1109
00:46:49,750 --> 00:46:51,550
(Hyun Moo can't help but laugh at Joon's typical response.)

1110
00:46:51,780 --> 00:46:55,680
I think the sirloin is chewier than the T-bone I tried in Korea.

1111
00:46:55,780 --> 00:46:57,350
- There's less oil.
- You're right.

1112
00:46:57,350 --> 00:46:59,290
- That's right. There's less oil.
- They're healthier.

1113
00:46:59,760 --> 00:47:01,290
- Really?
- They get exercise.

1114
00:47:02,190 --> 00:47:04,630
If the cattle run around in a large field, they're less oily.

1115
00:47:04,630 --> 00:47:06,860
(Pasture-raised Alberta beef with less fat and more bite)

1116
00:47:07,160 --> 00:47:09,160
People like meat with some oil in Korea and Japan.

1117
00:47:09,700 --> 00:47:11,500
They love well-marbled beef.

1118
00:47:11,500 --> 00:47:13,240
When you have the Korean-style grill...

1119
00:47:13,700 --> 00:47:16,170
with the pickled onions,

1120
00:47:16,170 --> 00:47:17,640
it's still nice.

1121
00:47:17,940 --> 00:47:19,410
I think it's the first time I didn't want leaf mustard kimchi...

1122
00:47:19,410 --> 00:47:20,910
while having steak.

1123
00:47:21,710 --> 00:47:23,550
I would usually want...

1124
00:47:23,550 --> 00:47:24,810
- dongchimi and leaf mustard kimchi.
- Right. Wasabi as well.

1125
00:47:24,810 --> 00:47:26,820
Yes. I'd want some wasabi...

1126
00:47:26,820 --> 00:47:29,150
to wash down the grease, but I don't feel like that now.

1127
00:47:29,320 --> 00:47:31,350
I guess it's because it has less fat.

1128
00:47:34,320 --> 00:47:36,860
- Since Joon...
- Yes?

1129
00:47:37,160 --> 00:47:38,960
wasn't great at describing the flavours...

1130
00:47:38,960 --> 00:47:40,700
and didn't know much about food,

1131
00:47:41,500 --> 00:47:43,370
he could make an acrostic poem with Calgary.

1132
00:47:44,000 --> 00:47:45,170
Let's finish it off with that.

1133
00:47:45,170 --> 00:47:46,700
One with Alberta and one with Calgary.

1134
00:47:47,740 --> 00:47:50,140
- Okay.
- One, two, three.

1135
00:47:50,140 --> 00:47:52,310
- First.
- I didn't know.

1136
00:47:54,310 --> 00:47:56,110
- Then?
- I waited patiently...

1137
00:47:56,110 --> 00:47:57,580
to finally taste this.

1138
00:47:59,150 --> 00:48:00,620
- And?
- I bet it'll still taste good,

1139
00:48:00,620 --> 00:48:03,950
- even a little burnt.
- You're good.

1140
00:48:06,390 --> 00:48:08,590
(Clapping)

1141
00:48:08,590 --> 00:48:09,660
You're the best.

1142
00:48:09,660 --> 00:48:11,560
- You could make one with Calgary.
- But...

1143
00:48:11,560 --> 00:48:12,560
First.

1144
00:48:12,560 --> 00:48:15,760
Hold on.

1145
00:48:16,630 --> 00:48:18,670
I came to Calgary.

1146
00:48:19,800 --> 00:48:20,970
He's different.

1147
00:48:21,400 --> 00:48:23,240
- Then?
- As I've said before,

1148
00:48:23,240 --> 00:48:24,510
it's my first time here.

1149
00:48:24,510 --> 00:48:25,670
- That's crazy.
- I had more fun...

1150
00:48:25,670 --> 00:48:27,280
than I had imagined.

1151
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:29,810
"As I've said before." That's genius.

1152
00:48:29,810 --> 00:48:31,150
You're a master of words.

1153
00:48:31,150 --> 00:48:33,280
One, two, three. And?

1154
00:48:33,650 --> 00:48:36,390
I'll make my exit while moving to the rhythm.

1155
00:48:36,390 --> 00:48:37,390
Rhythm, cue.

1156
00:48:37,550 --> 00:48:40,620
Calgary rhythm, yes.

1157
00:48:41,120 --> 00:48:42,760
He's making his exit so he doesn't feel embarrassed.

1158
00:48:42,760 --> 00:48:45,630
I'll get going. Come on out.

1159
00:48:46,360 --> 00:48:47,600
That's some effort.

1160
00:48:48,300 --> 00:48:49,700
I had a good meal.

1161
00:48:59,910 --> 00:49:05,410
(It's already their 5th day in Canada.)

1162
00:49:06,280 --> 00:49:08,750
(A slow weekend morning in downtown Calgary)

1163
00:49:11,890 --> 00:49:13,120
It's peaceful.

1164
00:49:14,560 --> 00:49:16,830
Where are we going now? It's our last day.

1165
00:49:17,260 --> 00:49:20,060
I realised we didn't go somewhere I always go when I travel.

1166
00:49:20,060 --> 00:49:21,260
Where is that?

1167
00:49:21,260 --> 00:49:23,000
I always go to this place anywhere I go.

1168
00:49:23,430 --> 00:49:24,870
You can know more about the people there.

1169
00:49:25,600 --> 00:49:26,800
I know where it is.

1170
00:49:27,540 --> 00:49:28,900
A convenience store? A supermarket?

1171
00:49:29,500 --> 00:49:31,410
Convenience store, supermarket... A little further.

1172
00:49:31,510 --> 00:49:32,510
The market!

1173
00:49:33,010 --> 00:49:35,110
- That's correct.
- I know you now.

1174
00:49:35,110 --> 00:49:36,410
- Why?
- I know you now.

1175
00:49:36,410 --> 00:49:37,680
I always go to the market.

1176
00:49:37,680 --> 00:49:38,680
Really?

1177
00:49:38,680 --> 00:49:39,880
Markets are fun.

1178
00:49:39,880 --> 00:49:41,250
- There are lots of tasty things.
- Right.

1179
00:49:41,250 --> 00:49:43,520
We're going to eat at the market as well.

1180
00:49:44,020 --> 00:49:45,090
Nice. Food at the market...

1181
00:49:45,090 --> 00:49:46,120
- is good.
- That's right.

1182
00:49:46,120 --> 00:49:47,360
What's unique about this place is that...

1183
00:49:47,360 --> 00:49:49,490
it closes on weekdays and opens on weekends.

1184
00:49:49,490 --> 00:49:50,690
Really?

1185
00:49:50,960 --> 00:49:53,200
So that's why we're going today.

1186
00:49:53,200 --> 00:49:54,800
Yes. That's why we didn't go there on the first day.

1187
00:49:54,800 --> 00:49:56,770
I see.

1188
00:49:56,770 --> 00:50:00,170
(The weekend market is open. Let's go check it out!)

1189
00:50:01,440 --> 00:50:03,040
I wonder if the fruits here are tasty.

1190
00:50:03,510 --> 00:50:05,210
I want to get some snacks too.

1191
00:50:05,310 --> 00:50:06,540
It has everything.

1192
00:50:08,180 --> 00:50:09,210
What?

1193
00:50:09,210 --> 00:50:11,050
There are suddenly so many cars.

1194
00:50:11,650 --> 00:50:13,220
There are so many people.

1195
00:50:13,480 --> 00:50:14,780
It's because it only opens on weekends.

1196
00:50:15,520 --> 00:50:17,490
Why are all the people crowded here?

1197
00:50:19,590 --> 00:50:21,060
Crossroads.

1198
00:50:21,060 --> 00:50:22,290
(This bustling spot is a weekend market.)

1199
00:50:24,060 --> 00:50:25,560
It's huge.

1200
00:50:26,130 --> 00:50:29,700
(One of the oldest farmers' markets, dating back to 1987)

1201
00:50:30,530 --> 00:50:32,100
(Packed with fresh produce,)

1202
00:50:32,100 --> 00:50:34,540
(harvested straight from the fields by local farmers)

1203
00:50:36,810 --> 00:50:38,510
(The best place to get a glimpse...)

1204
00:50:38,510 --> 00:50:40,540
(into everyday life in Calgary)

1205
00:50:41,240 --> 00:50:42,940
(Accommodation, Calgary)

1206
00:50:42,940 --> 00:50:45,480
(Farmers' Market)

1207
00:50:45,680 --> 00:50:47,020
Goodness.

1208
00:50:47,020 --> 00:50:48,080
It's pretty.

1209
00:50:48,850 --> 00:50:50,190
They keep coming.

1210
00:50:50,190 --> 00:50:52,490
- It's a famous spot.
- I guess they come with families.

1211
00:50:52,920 --> 00:50:55,090
(Calgary families spending the weekend...)

1212
00:50:55,090 --> 00:50:57,260
(shopping and enjoying a day out)

1213
00:50:57,560 --> 00:50:58,960
Look at the fruits.

1214
00:50:59,760 --> 00:51:01,130
There are so many of them.

1215
00:51:01,660 --> 00:51:04,030
It smells nice. What's this smell?

1216
00:51:04,370 --> 00:51:06,070
- I can smell it too.
- It smells nice.

1217
00:51:06,370 --> 00:51:09,140
- Tomatoes. They're big.
- There are so many of them.

1218
00:51:12,940 --> 00:51:14,180
Cantaloupe.

1219
00:51:15,140 --> 00:51:16,980
- This is tasty. Cantaloupe.
- What is this?

1220
00:51:16,980 --> 00:51:19,610
It's a cantaloupe. It's like a melon.

1221
00:51:20,550 --> 00:51:22,180
A popular melon in North America.

1222
00:51:22,450 --> 00:51:23,490
Melon?

1223
00:51:23,490 --> 00:51:25,920
The grapes here are small and cute.

1224
00:51:27,220 --> 00:51:29,060
- Peaches.
- It looks great.

1225
00:51:29,060 --> 00:51:30,790
- Are they soft peaches?
- Would you like to try?

1226
00:51:31,190 --> 00:51:33,090
- Would you like to try?
- I love soft peaches.

1227
00:51:34,660 --> 00:51:36,530
They're all soft peaches. I love it.

1228
00:51:37,830 --> 00:51:39,130
Who loves soft peaches?

1229
00:51:39,130 --> 00:51:40,500
- I like soft peaches as well.
- Me.

1230
00:51:40,500 --> 00:51:41,770
But I also like hard ones.

1231
00:51:41,770 --> 00:51:42,970
I only like soft peaches.

1232
00:51:44,110 --> 00:51:45,940
- This looks great.
- Okay.

1233
00:51:45,940 --> 00:51:47,080
Put it in.

1234
00:51:47,080 --> 00:51:48,480
The colour looks great.

1235
00:51:49,610 --> 00:51:51,050
- Aren't those too many?
- Wait.

1236
00:51:51,050 --> 00:51:52,110
I'll have around three of them.

1237
00:51:52,110 --> 00:51:53,280
Soft peaches...

1238
00:51:53,950 --> 00:51:55,650
I really love soft peaches.

1239
00:51:57,520 --> 00:51:58,850
What are these?

1240
00:52:01,320 --> 00:52:02,920
I've never seen these before.

1241
00:52:03,430 --> 00:52:04,960
Butternut...

1242
00:52:06,260 --> 00:52:07,360
- Squash?
- Squash?

1243
00:52:07,360 --> 00:52:08,500
What's a squash?

1244
00:52:09,030 --> 00:52:10,030
Squash...

1245
00:52:10,030 --> 00:52:11,370
It's this one.

1246
00:52:11,370 --> 00:52:12,870
(The three are baffled by the mysterious squash.)

1247
00:52:13,470 --> 00:52:15,170
Squash.

1248
00:52:16,300 --> 00:52:18,710
- It's a pumpkin.
- A pumpkin?

1249
00:52:18,810 --> 00:52:20,140
There's a pumpkin that looks like this?

1250
00:52:20,910 --> 00:52:22,440
This is funny.

1251
00:52:23,480 --> 00:52:25,310
(Butternut squash: A popular type of winter squash in Canada,)

1252
00:52:25,310 --> 00:52:27,150
(known for its sweet, nutty flavour.)

1253
00:52:27,150 --> 00:52:28,780
(It's used in a variety of dishes, from soups to risotto.)

1254
00:52:28,780 --> 00:52:30,550
It has a sweet flavour.

1255
00:52:30,550 --> 00:52:31,950
- That sounds nice.
- It's sweet.

1256
00:52:31,950 --> 00:52:34,160
It would be nice to use this to make pumpkin porridge.

1257
00:52:34,160 --> 00:52:35,290
That sounds nice.

1258
00:52:35,690 --> 00:52:36,760
Hello.

1259
00:52:37,830 --> 00:52:39,360
So they sell them by weight.

1260
00:52:39,830 --> 00:52:40,830
Thank you.

1261
00:52:40,830 --> 00:52:41,930
I want to try one.

1262
00:52:41,930 --> 00:52:43,030
Right now?

1263
00:52:43,470 --> 00:52:45,130
- I'll just have it.
- Shouldn't you wash it?

1264
00:52:45,130 --> 00:52:47,240
You'll just eat it like that? Like an apple?

1265
00:52:47,240 --> 00:52:49,200
- Yes.
- Shouldn't we wash it?

1266
00:52:49,200 --> 00:52:50,740
- The fuzz.
- I have lots of hair as well.

1267
00:52:50,770 --> 00:52:52,340
How is that...

1268
00:52:52,340 --> 00:52:53,680
(Laughing hysterically)

1269
00:52:54,310 --> 00:52:55,840
(It's so sweet that it makes him dance.)

1270
00:52:55,840 --> 00:52:57,650
Seriously? I've never seen anyone who ate it like that.

1271
00:52:57,650 --> 00:53:00,020
Me neither. People usually wash off the fuzz.

1272
00:53:00,020 --> 00:53:01,520
- I know.
- Won't your throat be itchy?

1273
00:53:01,520 --> 00:53:02,680
- What's it like?
- It's soft.

1274
00:53:02,680 --> 00:53:04,420
- Really?
- Really?

1275
00:53:05,020 --> 00:53:07,160
Can I take a bite?

1276
00:53:07,360 --> 00:53:11,030
Yes. It's not as juicy as the peaches in Korea.

1277
00:53:11,790 --> 00:53:13,430
It's as dry as the weather here.

1278
00:53:13,960 --> 00:53:15,960
- It's sweet.
- It doesn't drip, right?

1279
00:53:15,960 --> 00:53:17,030
It's sweet.

1280
00:53:17,330 --> 00:53:19,000
Grocery stores are always fun.

1281
00:53:19,230 --> 00:53:20,270
It is fun.

1282
00:53:22,140 --> 00:53:23,810
There's a lot of stuff.

1283
00:53:31,950 --> 00:53:34,550
(It's a permanent flea market...)

1284
00:53:34,550 --> 00:53:38,050
(filled with local specialties and handcrafted goods.)

1285
00:53:38,820 --> 00:53:43,430
(A weekend playground where families can come and play)

1286
00:53:46,130 --> 00:53:48,130
- It must be so large, right?
- Yes.

1287
00:53:48,130 --> 00:53:50,800
Right. It leads all the way to the back.

1288
00:53:51,400 --> 00:53:52,670
- After this...
- "Alpaca."

1289
00:53:52,670 --> 00:53:54,300
- After this...
- Yes, it's so cute.

1290
00:53:54,300 --> 00:53:55,640
I think it's alpaca fibre.

1291
00:53:55,640 --> 00:53:56,810
Alpaca fibre?

1292
00:53:58,410 --> 00:53:59,640
(They sell alpaca fibre...)

1293
00:53:59,640 --> 00:54:00,840
(from home-raised alpacas.)

1294
00:54:00,840 --> 00:54:03,380
They're alpaca socks, fur...

1295
00:54:03,580 --> 00:54:04,680
That's amazing.

1296
00:54:05,610 --> 00:54:07,820
This one's cute. It's adorable.

1297
00:54:08,580 --> 00:54:10,020
Those who like to knit...

1298
00:54:10,020 --> 00:54:12,790
can come here and buy the threads.

1299
00:54:16,830 --> 00:54:19,660
They have very different goods from Korean markets.

1300
00:54:19,660 --> 00:54:20,700
Right.

1301
00:54:20,800 --> 00:54:21,930
Do you like stuff like this?

1302
00:54:21,930 --> 00:54:23,870
What's this?

1303
00:54:25,570 --> 00:54:26,640
They're cute.

1304
00:54:27,940 --> 00:54:30,840
(The shop is filled with badges of different designs.)

1305
00:54:30,840 --> 00:54:32,410
Oh, they're old.

1306
00:54:32,410 --> 00:54:34,340
- Actual badges from the old days?
- Yes.

1307
00:54:34,580 --> 00:54:37,110
It's a literal flea market.

1308
00:54:37,110 --> 00:54:38,680
They sell old stuff.

1309
00:54:38,680 --> 00:54:40,320
- Vintage, right?
- Yes.

1310
00:54:40,320 --> 00:54:42,150
Look, these are old.

1311
00:54:42,720 --> 00:54:43,990
They're cool, though.

1312
00:54:44,590 --> 00:54:46,050
This is awesome.

1313
00:54:46,590 --> 00:54:47,860
They're old comic books.

1314
00:54:49,720 --> 00:54:52,290
(Comic books from the old days cover the entire wall.)

1315
00:54:52,890 --> 00:54:54,130
I wonder how old they are.

1316
00:54:55,900 --> 00:54:57,070
In 1993.

1317
00:54:59,570 --> 00:55:01,200
It's over 30 years old.

1318
00:55:01,970 --> 00:55:03,610
This is insane.

1319
00:55:03,810 --> 00:55:04,940
I'm going to buy some.

1320
00:55:06,170 --> 00:55:10,950
(Hyun Moo got three comic books.)

1321
00:55:11,350 --> 00:55:13,880
These are cute. Baby clothes and baby shoes.

1322
00:55:14,750 --> 00:55:16,520
- I think they're slippers.
- Goodness.

1323
00:55:17,450 --> 00:55:19,050
- So cute, right?
- Adorable.

1324
00:55:19,220 --> 00:55:20,820
- They're handmade.
- They're all handmade.

1325
00:55:20,820 --> 00:55:23,160
Yes. This place is fun.

1326
00:55:23,790 --> 00:55:25,260
I love watching stuff like this.

1327
00:55:25,960 --> 00:55:27,100
Things that you can find only here.

1328
00:55:32,900 --> 00:55:35,270
I'm hungry, so you can eat whatever you like.

1329
00:55:35,270 --> 00:55:36,270
Should we?

1330
00:55:36,670 --> 00:55:38,170
They have sushi.

1331
00:55:39,240 --> 00:55:40,640
I want to try them all.

1332
00:55:41,840 --> 00:55:42,840
(The place that satisfies the hunger of visitors...)

1333
00:55:42,840 --> 00:55:44,480
(who worked up an appetite browsing the market)

1334
00:55:55,190 --> 00:55:56,760
(From desserts and cheese to meat,)

1335
00:55:56,760 --> 00:56:00,730
(this food court has all types of flavours.)

1336
00:56:01,600 --> 00:56:03,200
They also have Korean fried chicken.

1337
00:56:05,100 --> 00:56:07,400
It's Korean fried chicken.

1338
00:56:07,400 --> 00:56:08,440
It is.

1339
00:56:08,640 --> 00:56:10,240
They have fried chicken and sushi.

1340
00:56:10,240 --> 00:56:11,870
Look what it says next to it.

1341
00:56:12,670 --> 00:56:16,410
(Next to it?)

1342
00:56:16,410 --> 00:56:17,710
- Japchae!
- Japchae.

1343
00:56:17,980 --> 00:56:19,050
- Japchae.
- Japchae?

1344
00:56:19,050 --> 00:56:20,320
- Japchae!
- Japchae.

1345
00:56:20,520 --> 00:56:21,750
- They have japchae?
- Japchae.

1346
00:56:21,750 --> 00:56:22,980
Foreigners like japchae.

1347
00:56:22,980 --> 00:56:23,990
Really?

1348
00:56:24,590 --> 00:56:26,090
That's surprising.

1349
00:56:29,690 --> 00:56:31,790
They have japchae in Canada.

1350
00:56:32,130 --> 00:56:34,130
I've never seen anything like it.

1351
00:56:34,130 --> 00:56:36,260
Let me go and buy japchae. I wonder what it tastes like.

1352
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:37,430
So do I.

1353
00:56:38,030 --> 00:56:40,070
(Meanwhile, Ju Bin gets a table.)

1354
00:56:40,800 --> 00:56:43,200
It's so nice to see a Korean store.

1355
00:56:43,710 --> 00:56:45,570
- Oh, they're Korean!
- Yes!

1356
00:56:45,670 --> 00:56:47,080
Oh, you guys are Koreans.

1357
00:56:47,080 --> 00:56:48,940
- Hello.
- I'm a fan.

1358
00:56:48,940 --> 00:56:50,610
- Oh, hi.
- No way!

1359
00:56:50,610 --> 00:56:51,850
- Nice to meet you.
- Hi.

1360
00:56:51,850 --> 00:56:53,550
- I saw Korean fried chicken...
- Hi!

1361
00:56:53,550 --> 00:56:55,480
- and japchae, and I was so glad.
- Right.

1362
00:56:55,480 --> 00:56:56,480
Are they popular?

1363
00:56:56,480 --> 00:56:57,890
- Our japchae is good.
- I wonder how it tastes.

1364
00:56:57,890 --> 00:56:58,890
- Japchae.
- Yes.

1365
00:56:58,890 --> 00:56:59,990
- That's what we're ordering.
- We have good japchae.

1366
00:56:59,990 --> 00:57:01,890
- I got so curious.
- You're really handsome.

1367
00:57:02,520 --> 00:57:04,390
- You're only looking at him.
- No, I'm not.

1368
00:57:04,390 --> 00:57:05,430
Okay.

1369
00:57:05,630 --> 00:57:08,730
Hyun Moo, you're also handsome.

1370
00:57:11,370 --> 00:57:13,100
We'll have a bowl of japchae and...

1371
00:57:13,300 --> 00:57:15,040
- Is Korean fried chicken available?
- Yes.

1372
00:57:15,100 --> 00:57:17,810
And we ought to try some of your kimchi.

1373
00:57:18,540 --> 00:57:19,940
- We...
- I can't wait.

1374
00:57:19,940 --> 00:57:21,180
make it every week.

1375
00:57:21,310 --> 00:57:23,280
- Oh, it's homemade?
- Yes.

1376
00:57:23,510 --> 00:57:25,480
- Do Canadians like it?
- They do.

1377
00:57:25,480 --> 00:57:26,780
Of course.

1378
00:57:26,780 --> 00:57:27,780
Really?

1379
00:57:27,780 --> 00:57:29,920
(Korean food is loved in Canada as well.)

1380
00:57:34,920 --> 00:57:38,730
(Their most popular item is japchae.)

1381
00:57:43,800 --> 00:57:45,830
- Thank you.
- Thank you for coming.

1382
00:57:48,900 --> 00:57:49,940
Ju Bin.

1383
00:57:50,610 --> 00:57:51,610
Ju Bin.

1384
00:57:53,210 --> 00:57:54,440
I'm so touched.

1385
00:57:54,580 --> 00:57:57,280
- Oh, my gosh.
- Awesome.

1386
00:57:57,280 --> 00:57:58,450
- It looks scrumptious!
- Eat up.

1387
00:57:58,450 --> 00:58:01,250
(A familiar spread of dishes in a foreign city)

1388
00:58:01,380 --> 00:58:02,950
Let's give it a try.

1389
00:58:03,080 --> 00:58:05,050
- It's giving me an appetite.
- Help yourself.

1390
00:58:05,050 --> 00:58:06,620
- I can't wait to try japchae.
- Right.

1391
00:58:07,320 --> 00:58:08,590
This is japchae.

1392
00:58:11,230 --> 00:58:12,790
I'm so excited.

1393
00:58:17,500 --> 00:58:18,870
(Moaning)

1394
00:58:24,070 --> 00:58:25,140
Japchae tastes good.

1395
00:58:26,910 --> 00:58:29,140
I think they use a different type of soy sauce.

1396
00:58:29,140 --> 00:58:30,140
Oh, it's good.

1397
00:58:30,140 --> 00:58:31,680
- Right?
- Yes, it's delicious.

1398
00:58:32,050 --> 00:58:33,720
- Their japchae is good.
- Right?

1399
00:58:33,720 --> 00:58:34,780
Yes.

1400
00:58:35,350 --> 00:58:36,950
But japchae here...

1401
00:58:37,950 --> 00:58:39,190
doesn't taste like a side dish.

1402
00:58:39,190 --> 00:58:42,020
- It's like main noodles, right?
- Yes.

1403
00:58:42,390 --> 00:58:43,590
I wonder what the difference is.

1404
00:58:43,960 --> 00:58:45,830
It's got more meat.

1405
00:58:46,230 --> 00:58:48,030
I saw them making it from scratch.

1406
00:58:48,500 --> 00:58:50,370
- Really?
- It was freshly cooked.

1407
00:58:51,430 --> 00:58:54,270
Non-Koreans look at this and think it's pasta.

1408
00:58:54,370 --> 00:58:55,370
Right.

1409
00:58:55,370 --> 00:58:56,940
- Really? This?
- Because it's noodles.

1410
00:58:56,940 --> 00:58:58,870
It's like a main dish for them.

1411
00:58:59,270 --> 00:59:01,340
- I once tried japchae in France, - Okay.

1412
00:59:01,510 --> 00:59:03,080
and they served it as if it was pasta.

1413
00:59:03,080 --> 00:59:04,550
I only got one bowl.

1414
00:59:04,710 --> 00:59:06,550
It makes sense, though.

1415
00:59:07,950 --> 00:59:10,150
(Now, a piece of kimchi from Canada)

1416
00:59:10,290 --> 00:59:11,950
I can't wait to try the kimchi.

1417
00:59:13,220 --> 00:59:14,720
It tastes different because the ingredients are different.

1418
00:59:15,060 --> 00:59:17,690
It's spicy. If you can't handle spicy, don't eat too much.

1419
00:59:22,460 --> 00:59:24,530
- This is my type of kimchi.
- It is?

1420
00:59:24,930 --> 00:59:26,130
I like it.

1421
00:59:28,700 --> 00:59:30,970
- It's not bad.
- I get why Canadians like it.

1422
00:59:30,970 --> 00:59:32,470
(Does kimchi taste that good?)

1423
00:59:32,840 --> 00:59:33,910
It's tasty.

1424
00:59:37,010 --> 00:59:39,280
(Now, a piece of mouth-watering Korean fried chicken)

1425
00:59:39,280 --> 00:59:40,750
This is japchae, right?

1426
00:59:41,550 --> 00:59:43,250
It tastes just like deep-fried boneless chicken.

1427
00:59:49,620 --> 00:59:50,630
- Yes.
- Right?

1428
00:59:50,630 --> 00:59:52,230
- It's the same.
- Deep-fried boneless chicken.

1429
00:59:53,090 --> 00:59:54,530
All their food is delicious.

1430
00:59:54,900 --> 00:59:56,560
I used to think...

1431
00:59:56,560 --> 00:59:58,600
that I'd be fine without Korean food in a foreign country,

1432
00:59:58,600 --> 00:59:59,830
but now that I actually try it,

1433
00:59:59,830 --> 01:00:01,100
- it's so tasty.
- It's different.

1434
01:00:01,100 --> 01:00:02,170
It's different.

1435
01:00:02,640 --> 01:00:04,470
- So, we picked the right food.
- Eat up.

1436
01:00:04,970 --> 01:00:05,970
It's good.

1437
01:00:10,680 --> 01:00:11,680
(Moaning)

1438
01:00:13,820 --> 01:00:18,290
(Ju Bin doesn't stop moving her chopsticks.)

1439
01:00:20,190 --> 01:00:21,620
This kimchi is so good.

1440
01:00:22,290 --> 01:00:24,390
We're eating at a far faster pace than usual.

1441
01:00:24,390 --> 01:00:25,890
- Right.
- Look at her.

1442
01:00:25,890 --> 01:00:27,260
I've never seen her eat this much.

1443
01:00:28,100 --> 01:00:29,360
Korean food is the best.

1444
01:00:32,200 --> 01:00:33,770
What are we doing next?

1445
01:00:34,800 --> 01:00:36,800
Have you been to the Korean Folk Village?

1446
01:00:36,800 --> 01:00:38,210
- You have?
- Yes.

1447
01:00:38,470 --> 01:00:39,640
We're going to a similar place.

1448
01:00:42,240 --> 01:00:43,280
(What does a Canadian folk village look like?)

1449
01:00:43,280 --> 01:00:44,650
It's so vast.

1450
01:00:45,680 --> 01:00:48,380
I want to try those places. Those little shops.

1451
01:00:48,380 --> 01:00:49,780
- It's fun to look around.
- Yes.

1452
01:00:49,780 --> 01:00:50,920
I loved the atmosphere.

1453
01:00:51,790 --> 01:00:55,390
(Tiny shops everywhere on the street)

1454
01:00:56,020 --> 01:00:58,130
(The weekend in Calgary couldn't be more lively.)

1455
01:00:58,130 --> 01:01:01,360
I should stop by Calgary when I'm headed to Banff.

1456
01:01:01,360 --> 01:01:02,660
You have to visit Banff.

1457
01:01:03,300 --> 01:01:05,130
It suddenly makes me wonder.

1458
01:01:05,130 --> 01:01:06,670
Why is Canada called Canada?

1459
01:01:06,830 --> 01:01:09,040
- I'll tell you why.
- Okay.

1460
01:01:09,170 --> 01:01:11,340
There was a man called Jacques Cartier.

1461
01:01:11,840 --> 01:01:13,940
He was the first one to land...

1462
01:01:13,940 --> 01:01:15,480
when there were only natives.

1463
01:01:15,480 --> 01:01:16,910
He asked them what the place was called,

1464
01:01:17,350 --> 01:01:18,850
and they said, "Kanata."

1465
01:01:19,210 --> 01:01:20,380
- "Kanata?" - It means "settlement..."

1466
01:01:20,380 --> 01:01:21,380
in their language.

1467
01:01:21,620 --> 01:01:23,050
That turned into Canada.

1468
01:01:23,050 --> 01:01:25,020
- I see.
- Really?

1469
01:01:25,020 --> 01:01:26,590
So, it's a country called "settlement."

1470
01:01:26,990 --> 01:01:28,620
That's interesting.

1471
01:01:28,620 --> 01:01:29,660
(History is more fun when you're travelling.)

1472
01:01:29,820 --> 01:01:31,530
(Farmers' Market)

1473
01:01:31,530 --> 01:01:34,230
(Heritage Park)

1474
01:01:34,230 --> 01:01:36,060
Welcome to Heritage Park.

1475
01:01:37,070 --> 01:01:39,400
- It's like the Korean Folk Village?
- Exactly.

1476
01:01:41,840 --> 01:01:43,570
(Heritage Park)

1477
01:01:43,810 --> 01:01:46,340
(It recreates western Canada from the 1860s through the 1950s.)

1478
01:01:46,910 --> 01:01:50,040
(A time-travel journey across nearly 500,000m²)

1479
01:01:53,450 --> 01:01:56,320
(Streets that bring Canada from a century ago back to life)

1480
01:01:56,820 --> 01:01:59,450
(Vintage cars straight out of faded photographs)

1481
01:02:00,190 --> 01:02:02,220
(A place where you can catch a glimpse of Canadian life...)

1482
01:02:02,220 --> 01:02:04,230
(from 100 years past)

1483
01:02:06,030 --> 01:02:07,660
(A cowboy's greeting)

1484
01:02:07,960 --> 01:02:11,030
(Welcome to Heritage Park.)

1485
01:02:12,130 --> 01:02:14,700
I love places like this.

1486
01:02:15,240 --> 01:02:16,900
It feels like a film set.

1487
01:02:18,170 --> 01:02:20,270
- It looks like Yeoju Outlets.
- What?

1488
01:02:20,440 --> 01:02:21,980
- An outlet mall.
- Did you say, "Yeoju Outlets?"

1489
01:02:22,480 --> 01:02:23,780
That's depressing.

1490
01:02:24,350 --> 01:02:27,280
Let's travel back in time to the 1930s and 1940s.

1491
01:02:28,920 --> 01:02:31,050
They must have filled gas tanks here.

1492
01:02:31,890 --> 01:02:34,590
- That's a gas tank.
- It's an old gas station.

1493
01:02:35,460 --> 01:02:36,590
A gas station from the old days.

1494
01:02:36,590 --> 01:02:38,090
(Vintage gas station from 100 years ago)

1495
01:02:38,460 --> 01:02:41,260
(Back in my day, we were fuelled there.)

1496
01:02:41,260 --> 01:02:43,200
- Old stuff always looks better.
- Yes.

1497
01:02:43,200 --> 01:02:44,630
- It's nice and small.
- Yes.

1498
01:02:44,800 --> 01:02:47,740
Everything seems like a spaceship nowadays.

1499
01:02:47,740 --> 01:02:49,070
- You're right.
- They're too practical.

1500
01:02:50,240 --> 01:02:51,710
(Honking)

1501
01:02:51,710 --> 01:02:53,440
How beautiful. Oh, look.

1502
01:02:53,880 --> 01:02:55,840
They even have a type of train.

1503
01:02:56,480 --> 01:02:57,950
(A tractor is pulling a wagon)

1504
01:02:57,950 --> 01:02:58,950
It's nice.

1505
01:03:01,280 --> 01:03:02,650
It's actually time travel.

1506
01:03:05,790 --> 01:03:08,560
This is an era from the show "Surprise."

1507
01:03:10,860 --> 01:03:12,730
Oh, horses! It's a carriage.

1508
01:03:12,730 --> 01:03:14,030
- Where?
- Look.

1509
01:03:14,400 --> 01:03:16,460
- It's a carriage.
- Check out those horses.

1510
01:03:17,100 --> 01:03:19,230
(A carriage strolls down the street just like in the old days.)

1511
01:03:20,100 --> 01:03:21,240
They're huge.

1512
01:03:23,170 --> 01:03:24,640
- So cute.
- Yes.

1513
01:03:24,640 --> 01:03:25,770
It looks like they're wearing boots.

1514
01:03:25,940 --> 01:03:27,210
Those are bear paws.

1515
01:03:32,280 --> 01:03:33,550
We're in the 1930s now.

1516
01:03:33,550 --> 01:03:34,680
(This time-travel journey feels more real as they walk around.)

1517
01:03:34,680 --> 01:03:37,320
- The 1930s in Canada.
- The 1930s in Canada.

1518
01:03:37,320 --> 01:03:38,420
So, it's almost 100 years ago.

1519
01:03:38,420 --> 01:03:40,290
They must have recreated the streetlamps as well, right?

1520
01:03:40,520 --> 01:03:41,560
Gosh.

1521
01:03:41,590 --> 01:03:43,490
Even the street lights are cute. Look.

1522
01:03:49,900 --> 01:03:52,400
(They spot a train track on one side of the village.)

1523
01:03:52,970 --> 01:03:54,440
There's an actual train.

1524
01:03:55,470 --> 01:03:58,870
(The old-fashioned train still functions.)

1525
01:04:00,170 --> 01:04:03,950
(The romance of days gone by unfolds along the old train tracks.)

1526
01:04:06,050 --> 01:04:07,620
(Staring)

1527
01:04:09,680 --> 01:04:11,290
That old man is staring at us.

1528
01:04:11,420 --> 01:04:12,450
- Where?
- Where?

1529
01:04:12,450 --> 01:04:13,450
- Seriously?
- No way.

1530
01:04:13,920 --> 01:04:16,220
(Welcome.)

1531
01:04:16,520 --> 01:04:18,030
Isn't that a mannequin?

1532
01:04:18,090 --> 01:04:19,190
Is it?

1533
01:04:19,360 --> 01:04:20,530
No, it's a real person.

1534
01:04:20,530 --> 01:04:22,130
Should we say hi? Hey!

1535
01:04:25,100 --> 01:04:27,100
(Surprised)

1536
01:04:27,100 --> 01:04:28,100
(Hello.)

1537
01:04:28,100 --> 01:04:29,570
I thought it was a mannequin.

1538
01:04:29,770 --> 01:04:31,710
(Hyun Moo and Ju Bin are surprised,)

1539
01:04:32,440 --> 01:04:34,640
(but Joon is pure and innocent.)

1540
01:04:35,740 --> 01:04:37,980
Oh, my. It was a real person.

1541
01:04:39,650 --> 01:04:42,080
They even recreated the old trucks.

1542
01:04:42,620 --> 01:04:43,680
This is nice.

1543
01:04:44,420 --> 01:04:45,550
How lovely.

1544
01:04:47,050 --> 01:04:48,690
This house is gorgeous.

1545
01:04:50,290 --> 01:04:52,190
It's your favourite type of house.

1546
01:04:52,190 --> 01:04:53,330
That's beautiful.

1547
01:04:55,500 --> 01:04:58,330
It's so fun how they decorated it the old way.

1548
01:04:58,470 --> 01:04:59,570
Gosh.

1549
01:04:59,970 --> 01:05:01,400
This place seems so old.

1550
01:05:04,110 --> 01:05:05,710
Is that a theme park that I see?

1551
01:05:07,740 --> 01:05:08,880
Gosh.

1552
01:05:09,410 --> 01:05:12,110
(A theme park right in the middle of Heritage Park)

1553
01:05:12,410 --> 01:05:14,150
Let's check out the old theme park.

1554
01:05:14,680 --> 01:05:17,080
They must have recreated it like it was back in the day, right?

1555
01:05:18,620 --> 01:05:21,760
(He's at a theme park, and he can't help but sing a children's song.)

1556
